---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/research-project.css';
---

<Layout
  title="DLCC — Deep Learning Cell Cycle - Luis Firl"
  description="DLCC: ongoing research developing a PyTorch model for cell-cycle phase classification from BBBC048 single-cell images."
>
  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">DLCC</p>

      <h1>Deep Learning Cell Cycle</h1>

      <p class="hero-text">
        Developing a deep learning model in PyTorch to classify cell-cycle
        phases from single-cell images. Using BBBC048, the project explores
        how learned image features can distinguish biological states.
      </p>

      <div class="project-context">
        <div>
          <span class="context-label">Research areas</span>
          <p>Machine Learning · Cell Cycle Biology</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span class="context-label">Status</span>
          <p>Ongoing research · Early development</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="project-links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/luisfirl/DLCC-Deep-Learning-Cell-Cycle"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Project repository (private) ↗
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://bbbc.broadinstitute.org/BBBC048"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          BBBC048 dataset ↗
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00623-3"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Dataset publication ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Planned workflow</p>
        <h2>From cell images to cell-cycle phases.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline" aria-label="Planned cell-cycle classification workflow">
        <div>
          <span>Data</span>
          <strong>BBBC048 single-cell images</strong>
        </div>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div>
          <span>Preparation</span>
          <strong>Image channels and preprocessing</strong>
        </div>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div>
          <span>Learning</span>
          <strong>Convolutional network in PyTorch</strong>
        </div>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div>
          <span>Prediction</span>
          <strong>Seven cell-cycle classes</strong>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-column text-block">
        <h3>Connecting appearance and biological state</h3>
        <div>
          <p>
            The central question is how much information about a cell's
            cycle phase can be learned from its image. DLCC aims to build
            a classifier that links image features to the annotated phases
            and examines where those distinctions remain difficult.
          </p>
          <p>
            The initial approach is supervised learning: each cell provides
            an image input and a reference label. A convolutional neural
            network will learn features for predicting one of the seven
            classes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading narrow dataset-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">The dataset</p>
        <h2>Annotated images of Jurkat cells.</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://bbbc.broadinstitute.org/BBBC048" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">BBBC048, version 1</a>{' '}
          contains images of 32,266 Jurkat cells acquired with the
          ImageStream platform, together with cell-cycle annotations.
          These labels cover G1, S and G2, as well as prophase, metaphase,
          anaphase and telophase.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="two-column text-block">
        <h3>From annotations to model inputs</h3>
        <div>
          <p>
            The source experiment used fluorescence staining to measure
            DNA content and identify mitotic cells. These measurements
            provide context for the dataset's reference labels.
          </p>
          <p>
            Selecting the image channels and defining their preprocessing
            are part of DLCC's initial development. Multiple images of the
            same cell must remain grouped when preparing data for training
            and evaluation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Model development</p>
        <h2>Learning features and evaluating what they capture.</h2>
        <p>
          The first model will provide a baseline for investigating
          image-based phase classification. Development is organized around
          four connected steps.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="outlook-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Image preparation</h3>
          <p>
            Choose the input channels, normalization and image transforms,
            preserving the cellular information needed for classification.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Convolutional learning</h3>
          <p>
            Build a PyTorch network that learns image features and maps
            them to seven class scores through supervised training.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Evaluation on held-out cells</h3>
          <p>
            Keep cells separate across training, validation and test sets,
            grouping all image variants of each cell. Reserve the test
            set for evaluating the final model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Performance across phases</h3>
          <p>
            Examine class imbalance with per-class precision and recall,
            macro-averaged F1 and a confusion matrix, paying particular
            attention to phases represented by few cells.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section ongoing-section">
    <div class="container ongoing-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current work</p>
        <h2>Building the first model.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="ongoing-copy">
        <p>
          DLCC is in early development. Current work explores convolutional
          feature extraction in PyTorch and lays the groundwork for training
          on BBBC048. A complete trained classifier and performance results
          are not yet available.
        </p>
        <p>
          The next milestone is a first trainable baseline, supported by
          consistent preprocessing and a reproducible evaluation workflow.
          Subsequent analysis will focus on which phases are confused and
          how model and input choices affect those predictions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="research-origin">
    <div class="container origin-grid">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research context</p>
      <div class="dataset-copy">
        <p class="large-text">
          DLCC connects learning from biological images with the question
          of how cellular appearance reflects cell-cycle state.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project uses{' '}
          <a href="https://bbbc.broadinstitute.org/BBBC048" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">BBBC048</a>{' '}
          from the Broad Bioimage Benchmark Collection. The images were
          provided by Andrew Filby and the Flow Cytometry Core Facility
          at Newcastle University. Images and annotations are licensed under{' '}
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CC BY-NC-SA 3.0</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="references-heading">Dataset and collection references</h3>
        <ol class="references">
          <li>
            Eulenberg, P., Köhler, N., Blasi, T. et al. (2017).{' '}
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00623-3" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Reconstructing cell cycle and disease progression using deep learning.
            </a>{' '}
            <em>Nature Communications</em>, 8, 463.
          </li>
          <li>
            Ljosa, V., Sokolnicki, K. L. &amp; Carpenter, A. E. (2012).{' '}
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nmeth.2083" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Annotated high-throughput microscopy image sets for validation.
            </a>{' '}
            <em>Nature Methods</em>, 9, 637.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .dataset-copy a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .references-heading {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .references {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .references li + li {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
